  "executive_briefing": "Global corporate tax policy and trade structures in 2026 are shaped by the operational enforcement of the OECD Pillar Two 15 percent global minimum tax alongside strategic domestic tax adjustments and escalating trade duty controls. While approximately 140 jurisdictions enforce GloBE rules to neutralize cross-border profit shifting, national authorities are recalibrating statutory tax mechanisms, cash grants, and targeted rebates\u2014such as Singapore's YA2026 CIT cash grants and 50 percent rebates\u2014to preserve domestic competitiveness. In international commerce, trade friction continues to escalate through unilateral tariffs, highlighted by a 12.5 percent U.S. tariff impacting a third of Singapore's exports to the United States. Concurrently, indirect tax administration is undergoing rapid digital integration through mandatory Peppol-based Continuous Transaction Controls (CTC), exemplified by Singapore's phased InvoiceNow roadmap extending through 2031.",
